README cartoon

OVERVIEW: Cartoon is the Top-Level Class for this project. It is in charge of
instantiating all higher object classes, as well as changing various universal
mechanics, such as the time of day and the sky's color depending on the time of day.
The program consists of 2 main panes along with a root, created by the PaneOrganizer class.
The background pane contains three important layers. The sky, city, and train. The
sky is a separate Class that instantiates and controls moon objects. Moon objects
include the moon, stars, and sun. Using trigonometry and several methods,
these celestial objects rotate. This is done through an event handler and lambda
expression in cartoon and sky that change the variable time based on arrow-key
presses. City is another object class. It generates random buildings in several layers.
Some have windows. Through a fancy javaFX method, interpolation, the buildings change
based on the time variable. Farther buildings change colors in accordance with
the day's time. The train is the final layer of the borderpane, and it includes
a station and a train that moves across the screen automatically every few seconds.
This happens using a timeline in the Mover class. Each train is composed of train wagons
from the train wagon class. There is also a button pane at the bottom that has
a quit button, and several labels, one of which changes.

PERSONAL CONNECTION: I'm from Chicago so I really wanted to recreate the feeling
of an urban environment, especially with moving trains. The city is beautiful at
every part of the day, so I wanted that to be shown.

SRC: By making each color high contrast, I increase the accessibility of the project.
The background sky creates an intense contrast with the rest of the scene no
matter the time of the day, and the moon/sun is nice and large.
Furthermore, I attempted to increase this area of SRC by scaling each label from
its default size. Because it is now larger, it should be easier to read
and comprehend.

DESIGN CHOICES: This cartoon implements several unique features in attempt to create the feel
of a lively and expansive city. By using random generation, the City class generates
random buildings with random properties. All rectangles in this program are created
using the MyRectangle class, which allows for greater control over the JavaFX rectangles.
The city is also meant to invoke the feeling of depth. Therefore it is composed of 3 rows,
each (except the front one) also has three more rows. The rose get darker the farther away,
the last row doesn't have windows. This successfully captures the feeling of depth.
It took me a very long time to figure out how to change the colors of the buildings
in accordance with time. For a while I attempted using the brighter and darker methods
that come with javaFX, but after researching I discovered interpolation which did wonders.

KNOWN BUGS: None

